"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[34454],{234454:e=>{e.exports='# Power BI DataModelSchema Structure\n\nThe DataModelSchema is the load-bearing artifact of every `.pbit` archive (and the standalone `DataModelSchema` JSON file). The agent navigates it by `regexFind` against the session-persisted file produced by `parseSemanticModel`. This skill enumerates the structural keys that matter for migration, the table-role classification you must do, and the M-query upstream patterns that drive `source-views.md`.\n\n## Encoding (handled by `parseSemanticModel`)\n\nDataModelSchema bytes inside `.pbit` archives are typically UTF-16 LE (sometimes with BOM, occasionally UTF-8 / UTF-16 BE). The `parseSemanticModel` tool decodes the bytes, validates them as JSON, and writes UTF-8 to the session folder; you never need to reason about encoding directly.\n\n## Key Paths\n\n- `model.tables[]` — all tables (filter out auto-generated `LocalDateTable_*` and `DateTableTemplate_*`).\n- `model.tables[].columns[]` — columns with `name`, `dataType`, `sourceColumn`.\n- `model.tables[].columns[].type == "calculated"` — DAX calculated columns.\n- `model.tables[].measures[]` — DAX measures with `name`, `expression[]`, `formatString`.\n- `model.tables[].partitions[].source` — M-query or DAX-table-expression source.\n- `model.tables[].hierarchies[]` — dimension hierarchies (informational).\n- `model.relationships[]` — join definitions (`fromTable`, `fromColumn`, `toTable`, `toColumn`, `isActive`).\n- `model.roles[]` — RLS role definitions (out of scope for the migration agent).\n- `model.expressions[]` — shared M-query expressions (the place where parameters and shared connection strings live; values are `null` in the schema and resolved at runtime).\n\n## Partition Source Types\n\n| `source.type` | Meaning                                                           |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `m`           | Power Query M expression (most common for Databricks-backed data) |\n| `calculated`  | DAX table expression (e.g. `{BLANK()}` measure-host tables)       |\n| `entity`      | DirectQuery / Analysis Services connection                        |\n| `inferred`    | Auto-generated by Power BI (e.g. aggregation tables)              |\n\nFor M-query specifics see "M-Query Source Patterns" below.\n\n## Table Role Classification\n\nA faithful migration depends on classifying every retained table into one of these roles **before** grouping measures into metric views. Check these conditions in order; the first match wins:\n\n1. **Internal Power BI** — `name` starts with `LocalDateTable_` or `DateTableTemplate_`. Skip entirely.\n2. **Measure container** — partition `source.type === "m"` AND M expression matches the empty-table fingerprint\n   `Table.FromRows(Json.Document(Binary.Decompress(Binary.FromText("i44FAA==", BinaryEncoding.Base64), ...)))`\n   AND the table has at least one DAX measure. The container holds **only DAX measures**, not data.\n   - Do **not** reify a measure container as its own metric view.\n   - Each measure inside fans out to whichever fact-domain MV its DAX expression aggregates over (see `architecture-patterns.md` \xa71 and \xa75).\n   - Real-world models commonly have multiple measure containers grouped by business domain (e.g. `A_Measures_R&T`, `Base Measures`, `_Measures`, `001 - Medidas`).\n3. **DAX measure host (calculated)** — partition `source.type === "calculated"` AND the expression is `{BLANK()}` or similar single-row-blank. Same handling as a measure container (fan measures out).\n4. **Config / lookup** — partition `source.type === "m"` AND M expression is `Table.FromRows(Json.Document(Binary.Decompress(Binary.FromText(<non-empty Base64>, ...))))`. Real embedded data (scaling factors, period selectors, version labels). Materialize via `createOrReplaceUCSourceView` with `selectStatement: \'SELECT * FROM VALUES (...) AS t(<col1>, <col2>, ...)\'` (Spark SQL requires column aliases on inline VALUES) only if a downstream dashboard needs it; otherwise omit. Use the column names from the M-type table declaration. See `source-views.md` for the tool contract.\n5. **Fact table with measures** — partition source is `Databricks.Catalogs(...)` or another real upstream, AND the table has at least one DAX measure attached directly to it. The MV\'s `source:` is this table directly only when the M-query is bare PASSTHROUGH navigation **and** the table has zero retained materializable calc columns (per `dax-patterns.md` \xa713.1); otherwise it is the table\'s `v_<table>` source SQL view, materialized in Phase B.2. Pure-`RELATED` (\xa713.2) and untranslatable (\xa713.3) calc cols do not by themselves force the table out of PASSTHROUGH — the former become metric-view joins, the latter cascade aggregating measures to `lod_fixed`. See `source-views.md` \xa71 and \xa7"Calculated columns" below.\n6. **Dimension table** — partition source is real upstream, has zero measures, and is referenced by `model.relationships[]` from at least one fact table.\n7. **Orphan / unused** — zero columns referenced by any retained measure, zero measures of its own, no incoming relationship from a retained fact. Omit.\n\n## Calculated columns\n\n`model.tables[].columns[].type === "calculated"` marks a column as DAX-defined rather than physical. The column\'s `expression` field holds the DAX. Calculated columns are evaluated row-by-row on the Power BI table **after** the M-query transformations apply — they are part of the post-M-query semantic-model layer that DAX measures, relationships, and downstream calculated columns all see.\n\nWhy this matters for migration:\n\n1. **Materializable calc columns push tables out of PASSTHROUGH — and only those.** A table whose M-query is bare `Databricks.Catalogs(...)` navigation classifies as PASSTHROUGH per `source-views.md` \xa71 — no source view, the metric view\'s `source:` is the raw upstream. The table reclassifies to **CREATE_VIEW** when, and only when, it carries at least one **materializable** retained calc column — a row-context-safe scalar shape per `dax-patterns.md` \xa713.1 (same-row arithmetic, `IF` / `SWITCH` / `DIVIDE` over row fields, scalar text/date funcs, null coalescing). Its `v_<table>` body becomes `SELECT u.*, <translated_\xa713.1_calc_columns> FROM <upstream> u`, and the metric view\'s `source:` becomes `<target_catalog>.<target_schema>.v_<table>`. A table whose only retained calc columns are pure `RELATED(...)` lifts (\xa713.2) stays PASSTHROUGH — the upstream already has every row column the metric view reads, and the dim attributes surface via metric-view joins (rule 4). A table whose only retained calc columns are untranslatable (\xa713.3) also stays PASSTHROUGH, but every measure that aggregates one of those columns becomes `cannot_migrate` / `lod_fixed` and the calc column itself is documented as omitted (rule 5).\n2. **Translate calc-column DAX once at the column level, not per measure.** Every aggregating measure then references the materialized name (`SUM(source.<calc_col>)`) instead of repeating the DAX-to-SQL translation. Calc-column DAX is **row-context** DAX, structurally different from the **filter-context** DAX that measures use — `dax-patterns.md` \xa713 enumerates the row-context-safe subset of patterns that can be expressed as a SQL derived column. Patterns outside that subset (aggregation functions, `CALCULATE`, `FILTER`, measure references, `EARLIER`, table-valued ops) cascade every aggregating measure to `cannot_migrate` / `lod_fixed`.\n3. **Materialize calc columns in dependency order** when one calc column references another within the same table. SQL handles this naturally if you list them in dependency order in the source view\'s `SELECT`; the agent works it out from each calc column\'s DAX `expression` (a calc column whose expression references `[OtherCalc]` depends on `[OtherCalc]`).\n4. **Pure `RELATED` calc columns are migrated as joins, not as derived columns.** A calc column whose DAX is _only_ `RELATED(other_table[col])` is structurally a join attribute. Use the join shape from `dax-patterns.md` \xa77 (the metric view\'s `joins[]` block) and expose the dim attribute via the metric view\'s `dimensions:`. The host fact table\'s `v_<table>` body adds nothing for this calc column — and if every retained calc column on a bare-PASSTHROUGH host is pure-`RELATED`, the host stays PASSTHROUGH for source-view purposes. Mixed shapes (`RELATED(other[col]) * Sales[Qty]`) only work cleanly as a metric-view dimension expression (`dim.col * source.qty`) when the calc column is consumed as a dimension; if it\'s aggregated in a measure, the only safe materialization is a denormalizing JOIN inside `v_<table>` — accept the denormalization or skip the measure with `lod_fixed`. See `dax-patterns.md` \xa713.2.\n5. **Untranslatable calc-column DAX cascades to `lod_fixed`.** Calc-column expressions that need DAX-only semantics (`EARLIER`, table-valued or row-context-dependent ops, complex multi-relationship `RELATED` chains) cannot be expressed as a SQL derived column. Mark every measure that aggregates such a calc column `cannot_migrate` with reason `lod_fixed`. Document the omitted calc column in `v_<table>`\'s `comment:` when a source view exists for the host (because the M-query is non-trivial **or** the host carries at least one \xa713.1 calc col); when the host is PASSTHROUGH with only \xa713.2 / \xa713.3 calc cols (no source view), document instead in the metric view\'s top-level YAML `comment:` per `SKILL.md` \xa7"Skip Documentation Format" and surface it in the Phase F final report.\n\nThe table-role classification in \xa7"Table Role Classification" is computed first against the M-query alone; calc-column presence then re-routes the table\'s bucket if needed (rule 1 above).\n\n## Reading the M-Query Partition Source\n\nEach table\'s `partitions[0].source.expression` is an M-query string (or a DAX expression for `calculated` types). The most common Databricks-backed and embedded shapes are below. For the full classification → source-view-strategy decision matrix see `source-views.md`.\n\n### Pattern 1: `Databricks.Catalogs` table navigation\n\nThe most common pattern for direct table reads from Databricks.\n\n```\nlet\n    Source = Databricks.Catalogs(db_environment, sql_warehouse_parameters),\n    db_database = Source{[Name=db_database_pbi,Kind="Database"]}[Data],\n    db_schema = db_database{[Name=db_schema_pbi,Kind="Schema"]}[Data],\n    pbi_my_table = db_schema{[Name="pbi_my_table_name",Kind="Table"]}[Data]\nin\n    pbi_my_table\n```\n\n**Key extraction points:**\n\n- Catalog parameter: unquoted `Name` value in the `Kind="Database"` step (e.g. `db_database_pbi`).\n- Schema parameter: unquoted `Name` value in the `Kind="Schema"` step (e.g. `db_schema_pbi`).\n- Table name: quoted `Name` value in the `Kind="Table"` step (e.g. `"pbi_my_table_name"`).\n\n**Regex to extract table name:**\n\n```\n\\[Name\\s*=\\s*"([^"]+)"\\s*,\\s*Kind\\s*=\\s*"Table"\\]\n```\n\nIf the M expression contains **only** this navigation pattern (no further filtering or transformation), classify as **PASSTHROUGH** in `source-views.md` — the metric view\'s `source:` points directly at the upstream table; no source SQL view needed.\n\n### Pattern 2: `Value.NativeQuery` (embedded SQL)\n\nUsed when the Power BI table runs a custom SQL query on Databricks.\n\n```\nlet\n    Source = Databricks.Catalogs(db_environment, sql_warehouse_parameters),\n    db_database = Source{[Name=db_database_pbi,Kind="Database"]}[Data],\n    db_schema = db_database{[Name=db_schema_pbi,Kind="Schema"]}[Data],\n    Result = Value.NativeQuery(\n        db_schema,\n        "SELECT a.col1, SUM(a.col2) AS total#(lf)FROM catalog.schema.my_table a#(lf)WHERE a.flag = \'active\'#(lf)GROUP BY ALL",\n        null,\n        [EnableFolding=true]\n    )\nin\n    Result\n```\n\n**Key extraction points:**\n\n- The SQL string is the second argument to `Value.NativeQuery`.\n- `#(lf)` represents newline; `#(cr)` represents carriage return. Replace with actual `\\n` / `\\r` when emitting the source view body.\n- `""` (doubled quotes) within the SQL string represent a literal double quote.\n\n**Regex to extract SQL** (matches the let-binding form shown above, where the first argument to `Value.NativeQuery` is a bound identifier — by far the most common shape in real `.pbit` files):\n\n```\nValue\\.NativeQuery\\s*\\(\\s*\\w+\\s*,\\s*"((?:[^"\\\\]|""|\\\\")*)"\n```\n\nFor inline call sites where the first argument is a complex expression (e.g. `Value.NativeQuery(Source{[Name=...,Kind="Database"]}[Data], "...")`), the regex above will not match. In that case, locate `Value.NativeQuery(`, count bracket depth past the first argument, and extract the string literal that begins after the first top-level `,`.\n\nClassify as **CREATE_VIEW** in `source-views.md` — call `createOrReplaceUCSourceView` with the decoded SQL as `selectStatement`; the metric view\'s `source:` becomes `<target_catalog>.<target_schema>.v_<table>`.\n\n### Pattern 3: `Table.FromRows` (embedded data)\n\nUsed for hardcoded lookup / config tables.\n\n```\nlet\n    Source = Table.FromRows(\n        Json.Document(Binary.Decompress(Binary.FromText("...", BinaryEncoding.Base64), Compression.Deflate)),\n        let _t = ((type nullable text) meta [Serialized.Text = true])\n        in type table [Column1 = _t, Column2 = _t]\n    ),\n    ...\nin\n    Result\n```\n\nThe table\'s data is embedded inside the `.pbit` itself; there is no Databricks upstream.\n\n**Two sub-cases distinguishable by the Base64 payload:**\n\n- `Binary.FromText("i44FAA==", ...)` → empty table (single dummy column, zero rows). This is the canonical **measure container** fingerprint — see Table Role Classification \xa72 above.\n- Any other Base64 → real embedded data (slicer-backing config, period selectors, scaling factors). Recreate via `createOrReplaceUCSourceView` with `selectStatement: \'SELECT * FROM VALUES (...) AS t(<col1>, <col2>, ...)\'` (Spark SQL requires column aliases on inline VALUES; preserve column names from the M-type table declaration) only if a dashboard widget needs it; otherwise omit. See `source-views.md` for the tool contract.\n\n### Pattern 4: DirectQuery `entity`\n\nUsed for Analysis Services connections.\n\n```json\n{\n  "source": {\n    "type": "entity",\n    "entityName": "FACT OM DATA",\n    "schemaName": "model",\n    "expressionSource": {\n      "name": "DirectQuery to AS - Weekly BPS Report"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nNot an M expression — stored as structured JSON. The `expressionSource.name` references a shared connection defined in `model.expressions[]`.\n\n**Migration strategy:** Out of scope for this migration. The Analysis Services source must be migrated to Databricks before the Power BI semantic model can be migrated. Trigger the 3-way upstream-resolution prompt (see `source-views.md`) so the user can supply explicit `<catalog>.<schema>.<table>` mappings or skip the affected measures with reason `data_not_in_uc`.\n\n### Pattern 5: Calculated table (DAX)\n\n```json\n{\n  "source": {\n    "type": "calculated",\n    "expression": ["{BLANK()}"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nA DAX expression that creates a table. Common shapes:\n\n- `{BLANK()}` — empty single-row table for hosting measures (treat the table as a measure container per \xa73 of Table Role Classification).\n- `DATATABLE(...)` — hardcoded data (treat like Pattern 3 real-data sub-case).\n- `SELECTCOLUMNS(...)` / `ADDCOLUMNS(...)` — derived tables from other relationships; rare. If encountered, surface to the user — they typically don\'t migrate cleanly.\n\n## Parameter Resolution\n\nM-query parameters live in `model.expressions[]` with `IsParameterQuery=true`. Their values are `null` in the schema — they\'re injected at runtime by Power BI Desktop / Service.\n\nCommon parameter names you\'ll encounter:\n\n| Parameter                  | Typical use                       |\n| -------------------------- | --------------------------------- |\n| `db_environment`           | Databricks workspace URL          |\n| `sql_warehouse_parameters` | SQL warehouse connection string   |\n| `db_database_pbi`          | Primary catalog name              |\n| `db_schema_pbi`            | Schema name                       |\n| `db_customer_master_data`  | Secondary catalog (customer dims) |\n\n**Generated SQL must always use concrete catalog/schema names — never placeholders.** The webapp flow executes the body of every source view directly through `createOrReplaceUCSourceView`; an unresolved literal `{db_database_pbi}` (or any other curly-braced token) is invalid Databricks SQL identifier syntax and will fail at parse time. Resolution rules:\n\n1. If the M-query is a clean `Databricks.Catalogs(...)` reference and the user established `source_catalog.source_schema` at the start of the session (or the default `target_catalog.target_schema`), substitute those concrete values for the parameter placeholders before emitting the SQL.\n2. If the user supplied an explicit per-table mapping (option (b) of the 3-way upstream-resolution prompt — see `source-views.md` \xa72), use that mapping verbatim.\n3. If a parameter cannot be resolved from the session prefixes or an explicit mapping — for example when the M-query references a parameter the user hasn\'t named — **stop and trigger the 3-way upstream-resolution prompt**. Do not emit DDL with unresolved placeholders, and do not invent a catalog name.\n\nThe same rule applies to metric view `source:` references and `joins[].source:` entries: concrete `catalog.schema.table`, never templated.\n'}}]);
